M-PLUS, not M.

> How do you address it? Or did it explicitly map the stack into both
> address spaces (seems wasteful, given the page size).

> Split I&D in V7 performed instruction fetches (and PC relative data
> fetches) from a separate 64k address space than data access. If you
> branched to the stack address in data space you would execute code
> from wherever that mapped to in address space.

RSX explicitly overmapped APR0 I and D so the MARK instruction would
work. 

As another detail of trivia, you could also have seperate ! and D
mapping of APR7 in Kernel mode and overmap the IO page with an
instruction page. 

