The ppdof text to PostScript print filter has the capability to load lpr
options from a file. The file must be located in /usr/lbin/ppd and is
referenced in the printcap entry. Keep in mind that most of the filter
options only apply to text files.

File Format:
-----------

% File comment. Do not use between *Setup and *end.
%
*Setup option_name/Comment listed on -Oprint page:
    -lpr option
    -lpr option
    -etc
*end

-------------
Begin Example
-------------

% Print a text file with 132 columns in portrait
% orientation. The print filter will auto-scale the
% font to make the text fit the page width.
*Setup PortraitWide/Portrait 132 columns:
    -Otm=0.5
    -Obm=0.5
    -Olm=0.25
    -Orm=0.25
    -w132
*end

% Print the text file in landscape with a
% page margin of .25 inchs.
%
*Setup Landscape/Landscape with .25 inch margin:
    -OLandscape
    -Opm=.25
*end

% Select tray with 3 hole paper and set the margins
% wide enough to not print over the holes for either
% single or two sided printing.
%
*Setup HolePaper/Select 3 hole paper:
    -Itray3
    -Olm=0.5
    -Orm=0.5
*end

-----------
End Example
-----------

Create the example file /usr/lbin/ppd/example.pfo and set the
protection to world read. Modify the printcap entry input filter
specification to reference the file by adding +Cexample.pfo.

For example:

    :if=/usr/lbin/ppdof +OPageSize=Letter +Cexample.pfo hplj8000.rpd:\

Enter the following command to print the options page.

    lpr -Pqueue -Oprint /etc/motd

If everything was configured correctly the printed options page
will list 'Setup=' options 'HolePaper', 'Landscape', and 'PortraitWide.'

To print a file using PortraitWide enter:

    lpr -Pqueue -Osetup=PortraitWide file.txt

The option PortraitWide is case insensitive.

Ernest


