   Some PDP-11 had hardware address spaces so small they were flat 
   (16 bit or less).  Those didn't use APR and such and the PDP-11 
   architecture didn't require them.  PDP-11/34 was one such little 
   monster that I used.

   For systems suporting larger hardware address spaces generally one 
   could can change the contents of the APR to get to the address in 
   question.  IIRC one normally let the overlay loader handle this for 
   memory resident overlays, but I don't recall that being a privileged
   operation.  Exception instructions vectoring to/from kernel mode
   could also change this so the OS didn't have to be in the same
   address space as the user mode code, but it was mode based memory
   protection which protected the code and structures of the kernel.
   We had trouble with DMA devices that didn't account for memory
   mapping when we ported an application to an 11/44.



