One oddity I have noticed relates to raster files. We regularly
deal with large imagery 100mb-1gb. These files no matter how
fragmented the system becomes tend to remain fairly contiguous and
according to "showfile -x" retain their Advfs I/O effeciency and
keep a lower number of extents. This does not apply to most data
generated from these images.

We also generate pyramid data sets of the images which basically
are the same image but resampled to multiple resolutions
all contained within one file. Each "level" if you will is used
for zooming into the image. These pyramid files themselves always
have extrememly poor Perf and Extents listings according to
"showfile -x".

The raster files are scanned on a remote station that dumps them
to the raid via NFS.  The pyramid layers are generated locally.
I do no understand why the images sent via nfs seem to be highly
resistant to fragmentation(?) and yet those pyramid files are not.

24_005 if a TIFF file. The _128 is a pyramid file of this same
image. The 24_005 TIFF was scanned to the raid and the _128 pyramid
was generated literally right after within the same 60 seconds.
-rw-rw-r--   1 foobar  users    363918990 Jul  7 13:09 24_005.tif
