The characteristics of the pyramid file seems to suggest to me that you 
build several pyramid files in parallel using several processes all 
writing their separate pyramid file to the disk at the same time.  This 
would result in the first writer getting a chunk of disk space, then 2nd 
writer getting the next chunk, the 3rd writer getting the next, etc...  
When the first writer goes to write its next chunk, it gets its next 
chunk separated from the its first chunk by all the chunks eaten by the 
other pyramid writers.  Thus you have a built in fragmenting systems.

The original TIFF file is more contiguous because you most likely only 
have the file transfer in progress and nothing else writing to the disk 
until after it is fully transferred.

But the big question is.  Do you notice a file access performance 
problem when you try to access your pyramid files?

Assuming I right about how you create pyramid files, then you have some 
obvious options.  

1) create one pyramid file at a time.

2) create pyramid files on a different disk, and then copy them to the 
800GB domain one at a time (maybe us symlinks to always point to the 
current completed pyramid files if you need to access them right away).

3) try using the migrate command as poor mans defragment.  It is not 
intended as a defragmentation tool, but the side effect of using migrate 
is that if there is sufficient unfragmented free space, it will tend to 
defragment the file to some limited extend just by the act of moving it.  
migrate can even be used to move the file on the same volume (multiple 
volumes are not required for migrate).  Again, migrate is not trying to 
defragment, it just sometimes happens to reduce fragmentation in a file 
as a side effect of moving the file.

4) use multiple domains, one per process creating pyramid files, so that 
each process is the only one writing to the domain.  Then use symlinks 
to point to the pyramid files so you can keep your current access 
approach.

5) This is a weak idea, but I'll pass it along anyway. Split your 
volumes up into more smaller volumes and have more volumes in the 
domain.  AdvFS creates a file on the volume with the most amount of free 
space.  If when the pyramid writers start writing the volumes in the 
domain are fairly well balanced (or at least several volumes with the 
most free space are well balanced), then hopefully the first pyramid 
file would get created on volume A, the 2nd pyramid would go to volume 
B, etc...  A file will always allocate storage from the same volume as 
long as that volume has free space to give it.  It will only get storage 
from a different volume if volume it is on has run out of space.  You 
could even start by creating empty pyramid files before the pyramid runs 
start and using the migrate utility to move each of them on their own 
volume so that they are allocating from essentially their own provate 
pool.  This assumes your pyramid applications will accept the empty file 
as its starting point and not delete it an start all over with a new 
file.  Again, then depends on having enough volumes so that each 
parallel pyramid writer can be writing to its own volume.  Later you can 
allow defragment and balance to move them any where you want.  You are 
just using this trick to get initially reasonably contiguous files.

6) upgrade to v5.1b and try vfast instead of defragment.  vfast is 
rather new and does not try to actually defragment everything.  Rather 
it trys to defragment just the hot files.  I have very little experience 
with vfast, so I'm not trying to push it, just making you aware that it 
exists.

7) other ideas that I have not thought of.

Then again, I could be totally wrong about how the pyramid files are 
created and thus my ideas are not worth the electrons they are written 
on :-)

Good luck.

